{
  "slug": "flatten-binary-tree-preorder",
  "title": "Preorder Flatten Order",
  "topic": "trees",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "trees / Preorder sequence",
  "statement": "# Preorder Flatten Order\n\nThe tree is given as a level-order array `root`: the children of the entry at index `i` live at indices `2i+1` (left) and `2i+2` (right). An entry holding `null` means that child is absent and is skipped while traversing.\n\nFlattening rewires the tree into a right-leaning chain that visits every present node exactly once in preorder (node, then left subtree, then right subtree). Because the judge compares returned values rather than pointer surgery, this task asks for the flattened order directly.\n\nReturn the preorder traversal of the present nodes as a flat array.\n\nConstraints: `0 <= root.length <= 30` and `-100 <= root[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "root = [1,2,5,3,4,null,6]",
      "output": "[1,2,3,4,5,6]",
      "explanation": "Preorder visits 1, then the left subtree as 2, 3, 4, then the right subtree as 5, 6."
    },
    {
      "input": "root = [1,null,2]",
      "output": "[1,2]",
      "explanation": "The missing left child is skipped, leaving the node followed by its right child."
    },
    {
      "input": "root = [3,1,null,2]",
      "output": "[3,1,2]",
      "explanation": "Node 3 comes first, then 1, then 1's left child 2 \u2014 even though 2 sits two levels down."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function flatten(root: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Preorder means node first, then the whole left subtree, then the right.",
    "A stack can replay the recursion: push right, then left.",
    "Null holes contribute nothing to the order."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Iterative preorder with an explicit stack: emit each node, then stack its right child below its left child so the left subtree always comes first.",
    "why_optimal": "Each present node is pushed and popped once, giving linear time with a stack bounded by the height. On 29 PAF-verified cases the judge measured the brute force at 0.29 ms and the optimal at 0.18 ms (about 1.6x faster).",
    "pitfalls": "Push the right child before the left so the left pops first; skip null holes entirely instead of emitting placeholders."
  },
